course: dictionary
lesson: 12712
title: A word with two faces
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their feel depending on who says them and how. Dork is slang, a casual word people use in everyday talk rather than in formal writing. It can mean a person who seems silly, awkward or uncool, often because they are very keen on something. It can also be a friendly, joking word for a lovable goof.
explain: Read the clues around it. «Ryan knows every fact about dinosaurs and talks about them all through lunch. Everyone rolls their eyes at him and calls him a dork.» The clues show a keen, awkward person, and the eye-rolling shows the word is unkind. Ask yourself: is the speaker being kind, or being mean?
explain: Now read this: «Dad sang loudly in the car, and I said, "What a dork!" with a big grin.» The grin tells you the word is friendly, so dork means a lovable goof here. The word is the same, but the tone and the smile change its meaning. Check the feeling before you decide.
fact: Dork is a noun: a dork, two dorks. The adjective is dorky, as in «dorky glasses».
fact: «Dork» can be a mean insult, but it can also be a friendly tease between friends.
fact: Dork is similar to nerd, dweeb and geek, but each word has its own feel.
fact: Dork is slang, and it is used a lot in American and Canadian school talk, films and cartoons.
fact: Slang words are best used in speech with friends; check with a teacher or parent before using one in writing.
exercise: «Don't be a dork,» said Mia when Sam fell over his shoelace. What does dork mean here? | A tall, leafy tree | A type of shoe | A kind teacher who helps everyone | * A silly, awkward person
exercise: «Dad sang loudly in the car, and I said, "What a dork!" with a big grin.» What feeling does the grin show? | Sleepiness | Fear | * Friendly teasing | Anger
exercise: Which word is the adjective form of dork? | * dorky | dorking | dorked | dorks
test: Which word is closest in meaning to dork? | bicycle | banana | * geek | sunshine
test: Why must you check the tone before you decide what dork means? | Because it always means happy | Because it is always a number | Because it is only ever a verb | * Because it can be a mean insult or a friendly tease
exam: Which sentence uses dork as a friendly tease? | * "You're such a dork, Ben," said Lily, laughing as she hugged him. | The sky had dork-coloured clouds. | The bully yelled, "Dork!" and pushed past him. | The dork bought a sandwich for lunch.
